en linked to a lowering the  absorption of dietary fat  b increasing glucagon-like peptide  1 aka glp-1 which is a  gut hormone known to improve metabolic  function  and c increasing angiopotent  like four aka ang ptl4 which  is a protein that plays a central role  in controlling fat stores  all of this is early research but i do  think we're just  scratching the surface when it comes to  fully understanding  probiotics and body weight never mind  the interaction between probiotics and  general health  now she also takes a multivitamin which  is something that she may actually  benefit from since  she doesn't seem to eat a whole lot of  fruits and veg  i don't believe that everyone needs a  multivitamin but if you're restricting  calories and therefore food  and your diet is severely limited like i  would say shayla's is  a multivitamin may help you fill in  those gaps  lastly she talks about taking an enzyme  supplement to help with her digestion  and this is where we hit a bit of a snag  so enzyme supplements are supposed to  help us with our digestion  since they are a source of the digestive  enzymes  that our bodies just naturally produce  in order to break down and digest foods  that we're eating  however unless there is a specific  health concern  at play which limits the amount of these  enzymes that we can produce  and or release there really is a limited  role for these supplements in the  average  healthy diet i spoke about this when i  talked to my colleague andrea hardy in  this video on bloating  but aside from vino and lactaid there is  little evidence to support combination  digestive enzymes  like these and you might think well  it can't hurt but i looked up these  particular pills  and found them on sale for a hundred and  four  dollars um yeah  that hurts of course as i've mentioned  before  there are specific situations where  enzymatic replacement  may be indicated such as in the event of  pancreatic insufficiency  cystic fibrosis certain types of cancer  such as pancreatic  colorectal and stomach cancers or after  gastric or pancreatic surgery  so if none of these ring a bell for your  recent medical history  you probably don't need to be  supplementing with pricey combination  enzyme replacement pills also if shayla  is finding her meals are difficult to  digest  this is why i would actually recommend  having smaller  more regular meals throughout the day  rather than just two  or even just three larger meals  now let's take a look at dinner and i'm  hoping it's not  another repeat  so boom here we have it oh i can't wait  to eat this so on a scale of  one to hungry right now i'm kind of just  at like a three  i ate my first meal today so late that  i'm not really that hungry  um but i need to stop eating so i can  start my fast  so there you have it okay so this is one  of the reasons why i don't always love  the intermittent fasting thing because  like she said she's not even hungry but  she has to eat right now because she has  to  finish eating before a specific time on  the clock  i can't imagine it feels really good to  be eating a  huge meal like this when you really have  no appetite  at all as for the meal i mean here we go  again we've got  rice chicken tiny bit of spinach on a  couple of tortillas  honestly it's not really surprising to  me that shayla doesn't  want to make time to eat i would  probably lack enthusiasm about the meals  too if it was always the same same  anyways after dinner she talks a little  bit more about her choice to fast which  for her is a solid 16 to 18 hours a day  so let's take a look at what she has to  say about it  i eat at 10 10 30 that's like normal for  me  but fasting will make  you snatched mark my words it will  you're gonna wake up in the morning  okay and you will be snatched you will  be all the way flat  um and i feel like that's one one of the  ways  to get abs is to fast which means it  doesn't mean you're starving yourself it  means that you're eating  all of your macros all of your calories  everything  in a shorter window and it works  period uh yeah  so shayla suggests that she's using  intermittent fasting  as the key to looking snatched and  waking up flat  in an effort to get abs now first of all  while we've already established that  intermittent fasting may help some  people lose weight  getting a flat stomach is not  necessarily  going to be the result of this now for  one  women naturally store more fat than men  do making it evolutionarily harder  to attain a six pack one hormone  estrogen has been linked to this  increase in fat storage in areas like  the thighs  butt and lower abdomen as a protective  and  evolutionary mechanism for childbearing  now another thing that i want to flag is  that shayla talks a lot about spot  reduction here so  wanting to have leaner arms or a flatter  stomach etc  but research suggests that this is very  unlikely  especially for women one study for  example had individuals train their abs  for six weeks  and while endurance and strength did  increase  there was no significant decreases in  abdominal  fat similarly another study had a group  trained their non-dominant leg for 12  weeks  and found that while the training was  effective at reducing overall  fat mass the reduction was not achieved  uniquely in that leg i also want to say  that it is  totally fine to have fitness goals it's  even fine for some of those goals  to be grounded in aesthetics i mean it's  hard  not to play into these pressures  especially in the fitness community so i  am not judging shayla  for just trying to survive in diet  culture  and do what she's been taught but i  would just have  loved to hear about some of the  non-aesthetic benefits of intermittent  fasting to her whether  that's because she's feeling more  energized or more satisfied  or her thinking is more clear etc  because if it doesn't feel good  i just don't know how sustainable it  really is  also if shayla finds that fasting helps  her to feel better i mean  that is great but it's not necessarily  safe or healthy for  everyone especially for women doing  these 16 to 18 hour fasts  research suggests that when women's  calories get too low from frequent fast  or extended fasts they may see a  disruption in luteinizing hormone  and follicle stimulating hormone which  in turn can increase the risk of  irregular periods  infertility and poor bone health now  while we don't  have a lot of research on humans yet  because intermittent fasting is kind of  a new hot phenomenon  early tests in rats have shown that just  three to six months of alternate day  fasting reduced ovary size and caused  irregular reproductive cycles  so something to consider if you're  wanting to start a family  and you're dieting heavily which i'm not  suggesting that is the case for shayla  but it may be for a lot of her viewers  who are watching  finally if i were to suggest any  supplements for shayla i would probably  like to see  a omega-3 supplement largely because i  didn't see  really any sources of omega-3 in her  diet  and maybe even vitamin d as well because  she  was probably getting a little bit of  vitamin d in the  coconut milk in day one and day two but  otherwise  likely not meeting her needs okay so  then on to the nitty gritty here  in general is this way of eating  balanced  well in terms of calories at 1885  calories and a goal to lose weight  i would say shayla's caloric deficit  would predict  around a half pound of weight loss per  week which is actually  a pretty sustainable rate so i mean not  bad there  her way of eating well not keto is  relatively low carb and higher fat  which is totally fine if that's working  great for her  having said that i would say she could  optimize her workouts  to keep the fat a little lower around  the workouts and then  spread her carbs and protein out a  little bit more throughout the day to  get  the most out of every gram my bigger  concern is really the lack of fruits and  vegetables in her day which  may prevent her from getting all of the  micronutrients that she actually needs  when i ran the numbers i saw that she  was only getting 19 grams of fiber which  is a little under the 25 gram  recommendation  and she's only getting two-thirds of her  potassium needs  both of these issues could be easily  remedied by  some more vegetables and fruit and like  i said it before  i would definitely love to see some  sources of omega-3s in her diet  but if she's not a fan of fatty fish or  she doesn't want to incorporate  things like you know flax or chia seeds  or  hemp hearts or things like that into her  diet then of course an omega-3  supplement  may be indicated finally in general i  would just love to see  more variety in her meals out of the  three days  there was literally only a total count  of 17  different foods plus condiments like  spices hot sauce and coffee  just 17. i mean meals don't have to be  complicated with a million ingredients  to be healthy  in fact whatever is easy and sustainable  is often best in my books but i would  just  love to see those ingredients kind of  switched up a little bit more  and that would help us optimize overall  nutrition  ultimately i would argue that the level  of meticulous restriction  and repetitiveness of this diet is  unlikely to be sustainable for  anyone long term and i would urge shayla  to try to find ways to make this  an enjoyable lifestyle before her  willpower to eat another ground chicken  wrap  runs dry now are there any problematic  claims or assumptions made by this  channel  well let's just say that if somebody is  sensitive to  diet talk and a lot of diet culture and  weighing  and calorie counting this would not be  a channel i recommend shayla is  obviously extremely driven by  aesthetic weight loss and she achieves  this through really strict diet rules  and rigidity  while i was happy not to see a channel  littered with extreme cheat day  documentation  she does talk about having a cheat day  once a week  to eat the foods that she loves and that  she craves so  this tells me that shayla could enjoy  food  and inherently does enjoy food and would  probably be a lot more enthusiastic  about  meals if she actually allowed herself a  little more variety  than those 17 different ingredients  also you know how i feel about cheat  days i mean  if we allowed ourselves enjoyment and  moderation in our day-to-day diet and  lives  there wouldn't need to be a massive  cheat day  not to mention it's actually really easy  to undo the caloric deficit that you  create for yourself  on a cheat day for example if you  created a 350 calorie deficit six days a  week  meaning you under 8 by 2 100 calories a  week  and then on the seventh day you had a  major cheat day and ate an extra 2 000  calories  the entire deficit that you put yourself  through hell and back for  in a week would be lost and that's not  even  like an always hungry kind of cheat day  situation  that would be a pretty modest cheat  this is why the concept i mean even  outside the moral language used to  describe it  just doesn't make a whole lot of sense  to me in the context of weight loss  another thing i didn't love was her  discussion on how intermittent fasting  will  flatten you out i think any time an  influencer is making claims about a diet  that they're following that has not been  supported by a large body of evidence  for the general public  i just think that they should have a  disclaimer that they're not a health  expert and that this is just their  personal experience  and their beliefs intermittent fasting  may result in weight loss due to the  fact that there is  often a reduction in calories and  insulin levels due to eating  in a smaller eating window but like any  diet  it's just not sustainable for most  people  now finally what can we take away from  shayla's channel that is  positive well first of all i will say  that all of shayla's meals are  definitely  very approachable they're simple to  prepare  they're featuring everyday ingredients i  mean there's nothing  crazy going on here which i definitely  appreciate  seeing also while a lot of shayla's  fitness goals are aesthetic based  to lean up which is fine and totally  fair as i said  she also has some goals that i  definitely want to focus on talking  about  one is that she wants to work towards  not tracking her macros  and also not relying on the scale for  progress updates  so often she also talks about how  everyone's health journey is going to be  different  and to not necessarily strive for  perfection which i love  in another video she talks about being  the best version of yourself  and while i think that there are a ton  of ways to accomplish this  outside of exercise and weight loss her  sentiment of  trying to not be so hard on yourself  throughout whatever journey you on  definitely may resonate bottom line  shayla's diet may not be the most  exciting  varied balanced diet and it would  no doubt feel super restrictive to most  people  if shayla is feeling good and strong  eating this way  and is not triggered by the extreme  measures that she's  had to take then i'm glad that it's  working for her  i don't want to project my own sadness  with this diet when she herself says  she doesn't mind eating the same things  every single day  as she mentions her journey and her diet  has been a lot of trial and error  and she has been trying to just find the  best way to fuel her body  that's totally fair but i would also  encourage shayla to find ways to support  her mind  and her relationship with food as well  of course i would love to see shayla  just like  space her meals out a little bit more  throughout the day up her fruits and  vegetables  and most importantly to increase the  variety  in her meals but i would also just love  to say that  there's no need to wait until she's  reached her goal weight to focus on  having a more supportive  relationship with food that might mean  weighing her food less often tracking  her calories  less aggressively and allowing herself  food that she's  actually excited to eat every single  day not just on cheat days so bottom  line  i think it's important to remember that  wellness is not just about what your  body looks like  or even how it physically operates but  that  you feel content and happy in your  everyday  well folks that is all for today's video  a big thank you again to squarespace for  sponsoring this video  if you liked it be sure to give it the  thumbs up leave me a comment below with  who you'd like to see me review next  subscribe to the channel  and i'll see you next time on abby's  kitchen bye  you
 

